    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d894">

                <pb facs="0945" n="933" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg933"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">361.1121 Pyk, Irena Teodozja/2: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-02-07T18:00:00-05:00">February 7, 1941—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>153. Your 223, February 5, 3 p.m. Since Irena Teodozja Pyk is an American-born
                    American citizen,<anchor n="24" xml:id="d894-f1"/> and since there is nothing to
                    indicate that she has taken any action which would result in her expatriation,
                    she is, in the circumstances, entitled to the protection of this Government.
                    Please, therefore, unless you perceive some reason for not so doing, accord her
                    every possible and proper assistance and keep the Department informed regarding
                    developments in the case.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d894-f1" n="24" xml:id="d894-f1-content">
                <p>She had been born at McKees Rocks, Pennsylvania, on January 31, 1918, and had
                    gone to Poland with her mother in 1928, residing in Lvov. Her father, who did
                    not go, died in 1938. Miss Pyk applied for a United States passport, and filed
                    an affidavit, in Warsaw on November 29, 1938.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
